  "finding": "Constant inputs agree bitwise, as prior receipts recorded. General inputs do NOT: the two kernels accumulate the k axis in different orders and binary32 addition is not associative, so bitwise equality is an artefact of the constant-input family rather than a property of the transformation.",
  "consequence": "The historical `max_abs_diff == 0` correctness witness is INPUT-FAMILY DEPENDENT and must not be read as a general equivalence claim. The Lean results are stated over Int, where blocking IS value-preserving (blockedSumObligation_holds); over binary32 it is not. Empirical agreement on general inputs is a matter of magnitude within rounding error, not of bitwise identity."
